  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nIt's a beautiful Thursday out there today for mid - going on \nlate - November. Temperatures early this afternoon are sitting \nin the lower and middle 60s beneath mostly clear skies. A \npersistent southwesterly breeze at 10 to 20 mph is in part \nresponsible for the mild weather. Conditions will remain quiet \ninto the night.\n\nA slug of low-level moisture will lift northeast across the \nregion on the edge of an impinging SW LLJ to our west late \ntonight into early tomorrow, primarily focusing into northwest \nand north-central IL before continuing into southern WI. \nMeanwhile, surface temps dropping into the lower 40s will yield \na stout temperature inversion just off the deck late tonight \ninto early tomorrow. Put together, this may allow for some \npatchy fog development through early tomorrow morning west and \nnorthwest of Chicago and especially outside of the metro and \nmore urbanized locales. 10 to 15 kt of flow atop the base of the\ninversion may bring the occasional light breeze down to the \nsurface, and scattered low cloud development with the moisture \nadvection further dampens concerns for anything too dense or \nwidespread. In the forecast, maintained a mention of patchy fog \nroughly near/north of I-88 and west of I-355/290. \n\nTomorrow and Saturday will be similarly warm to today with \nhighs forecast in the middle 60s to near 70 in spots. In fact, \nwe'll be giving daily temperature records a run for their money \ntomorrow at Chicago and Rockford where the record highs are 68 \nand 69F, respectively. The going forecast suggests we may fall \njust shy of those numbers, but it'll be close. Saturday's record\nhighs in the lower 70s seem a bit out of reach.\n\nWe'll see some periods of breezier winds over the next couple \nof days while that aforementioned LLJ inches nearer. Tomorrow \nshould see regular gusts up to 20 to 25 mph, particularly out \nwest toward the I-39 corridor. A nocturnal uptick in winds just \noff the deck is anticipated tomorrow evening which should usher \ncontinued breezy conditions well after dark, mainly in our west \nand north.\n\nImmediately behind this jet is a surface cold front which will \npass through the area on Sunday. Guidance has been on a steady \nslowing trend with the progression of this front and that has \nonly continued in this morning's 12Z suite. It now looks as \nthough rain should really struggle to reach the local area \nbefore the end of Saturday evening and highest coverage of rain \nshould fill in after daybreak Sunday. This front will introduce\nsome much cooler air to the region next week with high \ntemperatures after Sunday favored in the 40s, maybe even 30s in \nsome spots closer to midweek.\n\nDoom",
